## What Could Go Wrong

### 1. `[ngValue]` vs `[value]` type mismatch
If `[value]` is used instead of `[ngValue]`, `pageSize` (number) won't match
the option values (strings) and the dropdown will always show blank. Fixed by
using `[ngValue]` as shown above.

### 2. `pageSize` @Input passed by a parent
`pageSize` is declared as `@Input() declare pageSize: number` (line 146). If a
parent ever passes `[pageSize]="something"`, it will be set by Angular before
`ngOnChanges` fires, and our `loadPageSizePreference()` would overwrite it.
Currently no parent passes this input, so it's a non-issue. Leave the `@Input`
decorator in place — it's part of the public API — but note that if a parent
binds it in future, the localStorage preference takes precedence.

### 3. Corrupt / out-of-range localStorage value
The `includes(parsed)` guard in `loadPageSizePreference()` handles this: any
value not in `[12, 25, 50, 100]` (including `NaN`, old values, stale options)
falls back to 12.

### 4. Page number out of bounds after size change
When the user enlarges page size (e.g. from 12 to 50), they may have been on
page 4. Resetting `pageNumber = 1` in `onPageSizeChange` prevents the server
from receiving a page-number that no longer exists.

### 5. Cache invalidation
After a page-size change, `doSearch(true)` is called with `refresh=true`.
`checkCache` skips cache reads when `refresh=true`, so the server is always
hit. The cache is then repopulated via `cacheResults()` with the new page size.
No explicit cache invalidation is needed.

